<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ouyang Xiu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1007–<a href="/years/1072">1072</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ou-Yang Hsiu, Yongshu, Zuiweng, Liu Yi Jushi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ouyang Xiu was a Chinese historian, calligrapher, epigrapher, essayist, poet and politician of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, considered the central figure of the Eight Masters of the Tang and Song. Largely self-taught, he passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a> exam in 1030 and revived the Classical Prose Movement begun by Tang masters two centuries before, promoting it in the imperial examinations. A leading proponent of the Qingli Reforms of the 1040s, he was demoted when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-zhongyan">Fan Zhongyan</a> fell in 1045, and later headed the compilation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-book-of-tang">New Book of Tang</a>, completed in 1060, and wrote the Historical Records of the Five Dynasties on his own. He retired in 1071 after opposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-policies-song">New Policies</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ouyang-xiu.jpg" alt="Ouyang Xiu" width="300" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:宋参政欧阳文忠公修.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lyrics of the Song dynasty, by poets such as Su Shi and Li Qingzhao, and the prose essays of writers such as Han Yu and <b>Ouyang Xiu</b>, who revived an “ancient style” of clear and vigorous prose, are classics still read today.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ouyang Xiu</b>, whose father died when he was three and whose mother taught him herself, passed the jinshi in 1030 at his third attempt and rose to assistant councillor; Fan Zhongyan, who lost his father as a baby, studied in austerity, passed in 1015 and led the Qingli…</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mianyang, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Fuyang, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.47,104.74&amp;year=1049" aria-label="Mianyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7253 1018 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ouyang Xiu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7703 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7703 1288h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.47,104.74&amp;year=1049">What was here in 1049? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
